this is a production of cornell  university  the long island horticultural research  and extension center  is a campus of cornell university we've  been here in riverhead new york for 98  years  and our  purpose here is to help the agricultural  industry  succeed  there  is a lot of need for  up-to-date research  and assistance with growing plants  the impatiens is primarily of interest  to the greenhouse growers on long island  as well as some of the nurseries the  commercial the retail establishments  that sell plants  right here we've been breeding uh  impatience plants  trying to develop resistance to the  disease called impatience down in mildew  and um  there's always problems when you do  breeding and one of the one of the  issues that we found out with this plant  is they have what we call explosive seed  pots and it means when the seed pods are  ripe they explode and of course if  you're trying to harvest seed that's a  problem because then they're all over  the greenhouse floor  so we use we use a technique called  plant tissue culture to  assist our breeding program essentially  plant tissue culture is a way to grow  plants in a sterile environment on a  nutrient medium under controlled  environment so we will remove the pods  that we've pollinated on these plants  about 14 to 16 days after we've  pollinated them and we take those pods  to our laboratory  they're sterilized on the outside and  then they're opened aseptically to  expose the seeds and those seeds then  are placed in tissue culture to  germinate  the nice thing about that process is  that once they germinate in tissue  culture they're always going to be there  as a stock plant for us they're always  going to be available we can take  cuttings of those plants bring them out  here in the greenhouse if we find out  that we have a successful plant a  successful hybrid  we have the mother plant in tissue  culture free of diseases free of insects  and we can then rapidly propagate that  plant  to increase the numbers quickly  and these are the results of some of  that breeding research  so all these plants that you see here  are resistant to the disease we've done  field trials for them for the last two  years  but our next step then is to  produce  a plant that has a nice  uh looking flower and a nice symmetrical  growth habit so we are continuing to  hybridize these plants  and  we'll continue to  develop until we get a nice seed  propagating plants  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu
 

